- Expose what is already built & shipped: add `./common`, `./merge-configs`, `./presets/*`, and `./package.json` to `exports` in `packages/oxlint/package.json` (or stop shipping the internals).

- Re-export `PLUGINS`, `COMPATIBLE_RULES`, `getPluginFromRuleScope`, `getRuleScopeFromPlugin`, and `mergeConfigs` from `src/index.ts` so users don’t need deep imports.

- Narrow `tsdown` entry for this package to public files only (e.g. `src/index.ts`, `src/presets/index.ts`, plus any subpath you decide to expose). Stop shipping `get-built-in-rules-meta`, `get-js-rules-meta`, `get-referenced-rule-names`, `get-rules-meta`, `get-compatible-rules`, `get-config-referenced-rule-names` — they are dev-only and one of them calls `spawnSync("npx", ["oxlint", ...])`.

- Add an escape hatch on `defineConfig` for `options.typeAware`/`options.typeCheck`. Today `core` hard-codes both to `true` with no opt-out, which forces tsgolint on every consumer.

- Return a lighter type from `defineConfig`. `DefinedConfig<T>` deep-simplifies a left-fold of `MergeConfigs` over 1300+ rule literals; returning `OxlintConfig` (or a shallower alias) would cut consumer TypeScript perf cost substantially.

- Tighten `OxlintConfig["rules"]` to a `Rules` type built from `PLUGINS` × rule-value literals so that rule names autocomplete instead of falling back to `DummyRuleMap`.
